<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Horn and East Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Barawa</h1><p class="st-meta">1100 CE – 1871 CE (approx.) · capital Barawa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small Somali port city known for its Islamic scholarship and its own Bantu language, Chimiini, spoken by the Bravanese.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Barawa, or Brava, developed as a coastal trading town on the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> coast in the early second millennium, settled by Somali Tunni clans together with Arab and Swahili-speaking merchants. Arab geographers mention it from around the 12th century, and its people developed the distinct Chimiini dialect of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The town was governed by its leading families and religious notables rather than a strong monarchy. It traded hides, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and grain from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shebelle-river">Shabelle</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jubba-river">Jubba</a> valleys, and was famous as a centre of Islamic learning, later linked with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qadiriyya">Qadiriyya</a> brotherhood. It resisted the Portuguese, who sacked it in 1506, and was for long tributary to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ajuran-sultanate">Ajuran Sultanate</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After Ajuran's decline Barawa came under Omani influence and in 1871 under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sultanate-of-zanzibar">Sultanate of Zanzibar</a>. Zanzibar leased the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benadir">Benadir</a> ports to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and Barawa became part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/italian-somaliland">Italian Somaliland</a>. Its Bravanese community survives, though much reduced by the civil war after 1991.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/barawa.jpg" alt="Barawa" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: FaarisAdam · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chilaani_Lighthouse_in_Baraawe.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1100 CE – 1871 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Barawa</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Chimiini, Somali</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/somalia">Somalia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Brava, Sultanate of Barawa</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1486&amp;focus=barawa" aria-label="Barawa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5903 2136 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Barawa lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6223 2392L6202 2325M6223 2392L6086 2664"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6202 2325h0M6086 2664h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6223 2392h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6223 2392h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1486&amp;focus=barawa">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

